    "status": "confirmed",
    "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured Math Academy solutions. q-87828: divide by 5, take logarithms, and reverse the inequality because ln(4/9)<0. q-115230: a positive exponential cannot be at most -12, so there are no solutions. q-87893: the exponential is positive, so the original expression exceeds 6 for every real x. q-115250: logarithms give x(ln5-ln2)<ln5-2ln2; the coefficient is positive. q-365323: logarithms give x(3ln5-2ln4)≤ln3+2ln5-ln4; the coefficient ln(125/16) is positive. q-88011: the logarithmic inequality gives x<254, intersected with its domain x>-2. q-102968: it gives x<-13/7, intersected with x>-2. q-102983: monotonicity gives x≥1/4, while both logarithms require -9<x<10/3. q-87745: exponentiation gives x≤2/(3e^5-1), and the logarithmic domain requires x>0. q-89651: the denominator is always positive, so cancellation gives 3x≤4. q-89661: factoring gives 3x(2x^4+5)>0, equivalent to x>0. q-89676: the quadratic factor is always negative, so x-1>0. q-116269: arctan(x)-π is always negative, so multiplication reverses the inequality and gives 9x≤-1. q-89571: for x>3 the numerator and denominator are positive, so every allowed x works. Its source prose changes the intermediate condition x≥3/2 to x>3/2; this harmless strictness error does not change the intersection or final answer. q-73455: only III is a closed bounded interval on which the plotted function is continuous; I contains the discontinuity at zero and II excludes an endpoint. q-127040: only I is closed and bounded with a continuous function; II is open and III is unbounded.",
